# =========================================================== abstract
d.heading("Abstract", 2)
ABS = (f"**Purpose.** Governments mandate accrual accounting and performance-based budgeting, yet adoption on paper often fails to change decisions. This study asks how coercive, normative and mimetic pressures push executive agencies towards substantive implementation or symbolic compliance, how these responses affect financial accountability through the usefulness of reported information, and whether the maturity of the financial information system conditions the process. "
       f"**Design/methodology/approach.** A survey of {n} financial managers, comptrollers, budget experts and accountants in executive agencies was analysed with covariance-based structural equation modelling in which every item loads only on its own latent variable (no composites), latent interaction terms, and 2,000-draw bootstrap estimates of conditional indirect effects. "
       f"**Findings.** Coercive pressure raised both substantive implementation (β = {f2(PA['SUB~COE']['beta'])}) and symbolic compliance (β = {f2(PA['SYM~COE']['beta'])}); normative pressure raised the former (β = {f2(PA['SUB~NOR']['beta'])}) and lowered the latter (β = {f2(PA['SYM~NOR']['beta'])}); mimetic pressure raised symbolic compliance only (β = {f2(PA['SYM~MIM']['beta'])}). Substantive implementation increased, and symbolic compliance reduced, both the usefulness of information and accountability, and usefulness mediated part of these effects. Maturity amplified the usefulness payoff of substantive implementation and dampened the translation of coercion into symbolic compliance. The net effect of coercion was close to zero because its constructive and ceremonial routes offset one another, while normative pressure had a clear positive net effect. "
       f"**Originality/value.** The study separates substantive from symbolic responses to the same pressures and shows when mandates translate into accountability. "
       f"**Practical implications.** Reform agencies should supplement mandates with professional norms, monitor use rather than submission of reports, and invest in integrated, auditable systems.")
d.para(ABS, indent=False, size=22, after=100)
d.para("**Keywords:** institutional theory; decoupling; accrual accounting; performance-based budgeting; public-sector accountability; information systems", indent=False, size=22, after=40)
d.para("**JEL classification:** M41; M48; H83; D73", indent=False, size=22, after=40)
d.para("**Paper type:** Research paper", indent=False, size=22, after=120)
# =========================================================== 1 Introduction
d.heading("1. Introduction")
d.para(f"Over the past four decades, public-sector accounting and budgeting have been reorganised around the idea that governments should manage by information on costs, assets, liabilities and results rather than by cash and appropriations {ca('hood1991', 'lapsley2009')}. Accrual accounting, internationally promoted through the International Public Sector Accounting Standards, and performance-based budgeting, which links appropriations to outputs and outcomes, are the two flagship instruments of this agenda {ca('pollitt2017', 'christiaens2015')}. Their promise is better decisions and stronger accountability to legislatures, auditors and citizens.")
d.para(f"The evidence on delivery is mixed. Cross-country studies show that countries adopt the same standards in markedly different ways {ca('pina2009', 'christiaens2010')}, and the literature on performance management documents that producing performance information is not the same as using it {ca('moynihan2008', 'moynihan2010', 'kroll2015')}. Iran illustrates the gap. Public-sector accounting standards and performance-based budgeting have been introduced through legislation and development plans, but recent Iranian studies report partial implementation, legal and structural obstacles, and weak use of information: {cn('ahmadi')} estimate that performance-based budgeting is implemented at roughly 34, 26 and 31 per cent in strategic planning, cost analysis and performance management respectively, and {cn('nourani')} identify legal, structural and environmental bottlenecks to implementing public-sector accounting standards. Qualitative models {ca('pourghaffar', 'naroui')} map what a good system should contain, but we know little about what drives agencies to implement reforms substantively rather than ceremonially, and what each response costs or yields in accountability.")
d.para(f"Institutional theory offers a precise language for this puzzle. Organisations facing coercive, normative and mimetic pressures adopt structures to gain legitimacy {ca('dimaggio1983')}, and formal adoption may be decoupled from operations {ca('meyer1977', 'bromley2012')}. Yet three gaps remain. First, empirical accounting studies rarely treat substantive implementation and symbolic compliance as two distinct outcomes of the same pressures; they tend to measure “adoption” as a single construct. Second, the mechanism linking implementation to accountability—whether reported information is actually useful to those who decide—has seldom been modelled. Third, the role of the information infrastructure in making ceremonial behaviour visible, and substantive behaviour productive, is under-theorised, despite the emphasis on integrated digital systems in public-management reform {ca('dunleavy2006', 'delone2003')}.")
d.para("This paper addresses these gaps with a survey of financial and budget staff in Iranian executive agencies. It makes three contributions. (i) It shows that institutional pressure is not a single cause with a single effect: coercion drives both substantive and symbolic responses, normative pressure favours substance, and mimetic pressure breeds form. (ii) It identifies usefulness of reported information as the bridge from implementation to accountability and quantifies the offsetting routes that make the net effect of coercion small. (iii) It demonstrates that information-system maturity conditions both the payoff of substantive implementation and the pull of coercion towards ceremony. Methodologically, the study estimates a fully latent model with latent interactions and bootstrap conditional indirect effects instead of the summed or averaged scale scores that dominate the field.")
d.para("The remainder of the paper is organised as follows. Section 2 develops the theory and hypotheses; Section 3 describes data, measures and methods; Section 4 reports results, including competing models and robustness checks; Section 5 discusses theoretical, practical and policy implications; Section 6 outlines limitations and future research. Appendices report the full instrument, measurement and structural estimates, and supplementary analyses.")
# =========================================================== 2 Theory
d.heading("2. Theory and hypotheses")
d.heading("2.1 Institutional pressures and organisational responses", 2)
d.para(f"{cn('dimaggio1983')} distinguish three mechanisms of institutional isomorphism. Coercive pressure stems from formal and informal pressures exerted by organisations on which an agency depends, and from legal mandates; normative pressure arises from professionalisation, training and occupational networks; mimetic pressure arises when uncertainty leads organisations to imitate peers perceived as legitimate. {cn('scott2014')} links these mechanisms to the regulative, normative and cultural-cognitive pillars of institutions. Empirically the three are correlated but not interchangeable, and research on their effects shows that their consequences differ {ca('mizruchi1999', 'teo2003')}.")
d.para(f"Organisations are not passive recipients. {cn('oliver1991')} describes a repertoire of strategic responses from acquiescence to avoidance, and {cn('kostova2002')} separate ceremonial adoption—the practice is adopted formally without understanding or commitment—from internalisation, in which employees value and use it. In public accounting, performance measures and accrual statements are particularly prone to ceremonial use because they are visible to superiors, legible as signals of modernisation, and costly to verify {ca('brignall2000', 'modell2009')}. We therefore define two organisational responses. **Substantive implementation** is the use of programme structures, cost information and accrual statements in preparing budgets and in managerial decisions. **Symbolic compliance** is the production of reports and indicators mainly for submission to higher authorities, with indicators that are easy to meet and little effect on resource allocation.")
d.heading("2.2 Pressures and responses (H1–H5)", 2)
d.para(f"Coercive pressure makes non-adoption costly and behaviour observable. Where legal mandates and oversight are strong, agencies have reasons both to implement and to display implementation {ca('dimaggio1983', 'oliver1991')}. Because mandates do not distinguish between the two, we expect coercion to raise both responses (H1, H2). Normative pressure operates by shaping professional identity: accountants and comptrollers who see accrual information as a mark of professional competence internalise it, which should raise substantive implementation and reduce reliance on ritual (H3, H4) {ca('dimaggio1983', 'greenwood2011')}. Mimetic pressure, by contrast, supplies templates; imitation is cheap, copies form rather than function, and is most attractive when the agency lacks the capacity or incentive to internalise. We therefore expect mimetic pressure to raise symbolic compliance (H5) but not to promote substantive implementation, a path we estimate but do not hypothesise.")
d.para("**H1:** Coercive pressure is positively related to substantive implementation.  **H2:** Coercive pressure is positively related to symbolic compliance.  **H3:** Normative pressure is positively related to substantive implementation.  **H4:** Normative pressure is negatively related to symbolic compliance.  **H5:** Mimetic pressure is positively related to symbolic compliance.", indent=False)
d.heading("2.3 From implementation to usefulness and accountability (H6–H10)", 2)
d.para(f"Reforms create value only when information is used {ca('moynihan2008', 'moynihan2010')}. When agencies implement substantively, cost and accrual information enters budget preparation and management meetings and is therefore timely, understandable and relevant; when compliance is symbolic, reports are produced for form, are less trusted internally and are discounted by users {ca('kroll2015', 'modell2009')}. We define the usefulness of reported information as the extent to which information is the basis of managerial decisions, is timely and understandable, gives a fuller picture than cash accounting and is reliable for evaluating efficiency and effectiveness. Accountability, following {cn('bovens2007')}, is a relationship in which the actor informs, justifies and accepts consequences; it is also a core input to public value {ca('moore1995', 'bracci2015')}. Substantive implementation should strengthen accountability directly, by making deviations traceable and responsibilities clear, and indirectly, through usefulness; symbolic compliance should weaken it by providing information that cannot be verified or acted upon.")
d.para("**H6:** Substantive implementation is positively related to usefulness of information.  **H7:** Symbolic compliance is negatively related to usefulness of information.  **H8:** Substantive implementation is positively related to financial accountability.  **H9:** Symbolic compliance is negatively related to financial accountability.  **H10:** Usefulness of information is positively related to financial accountability.", indent=False)
d.heading("2.4 The conditioning role of information-system maturity (H11–H12)", 2)
d.para(f"Information-systems research holds that the benefits of information depend on system and information quality {ca('delone2003')}, and digital-era governance stresses integration, reintegration of functions and transparency {ca('dunleavy2006')}. We define maturity as the integration, real-time availability and auditability of the agency's financial systems. Maturity should play two roles. First, integrated and timely data allow substantive implementation to deliver usable information; maturity should therefore strengthen the effect of substantive implementation on usefulness (H11). Second, auditable trails and traceable changes raise the cost of reporting that is detached from practice; maturity should therefore weaken the pathway from coercive pressure to symbolic compliance, because mandated reporting becomes verifiable rather than ceremonial (H12).")
d.para("**H11:** Information-system maturity strengthens the positive effect of substantive implementation on usefulness of information.  **H12:** Information-system maturity weakens the positive effect of coercive pressure on symbolic compliance.  **H13 (mediation):** Institutional pressures exert indirect effects on accountability through substantive implementation and symbolic compliance, via usefulness.", indent=False)
d.figure(fig(1), "Figure 1. Conceptual model. Solid arrows: structural paths; dashed orange arrows: moderation by information-system maturity.", 15.5)
# =========================================================== 3 Method
d.heading("3. Data and method")
d.heading("3.1 Context, sample and procedure", 2)
dm = R["demo"]
d.para(f"The study population comprises financial managers, comptrollers, budget experts and accountants or internal auditors of executive agencies. The analysed sample has {n} respondents: {dm['org']['1']:.0f}% from ministries and national organisations, {dm['org']['2']:.0f}% from universities and health-care institutions, {dm['org']['3']:.0f}% from municipalities and local bodies and {dm['org']['4']:.0f}% from provincial offices; the mean professional experience is {dm['exp_m']:.1f} years (SD = {dm['exp_sd']:.1f}) and the mean age {dm['age_m']:.1f} years (SD = {dm['age_sd']:.1f}). ==[Sampling procedure, fieldwork period, distribution channel and response rate must be completed from the actual administration.]== Respondents rated the agency in which they work; anonymity was assured and answers are analysed in aggregate.")
d.para(f"With {R['n_params_sem']} free parameters the ratio of observations to parameters is {n/R['n_params_sem']:.1f}:1, below the conventional 10:1 rule of thumb. We therefore base the adequacy of the sample on power analysis for covariance-structure models {ca('maccallum1996')}: for the degrees of freedom of the structural model, power to reject close fit (RMSEA = .05) against RMSEA = .08 exceeds .99. Because latent variables with multiple reflective indicators absorb measurement error, and the loadings are high, we regard N = {n} as adequate for the model, but we report DWLS and trimmed-sample estimates as checks.")
d.heading("3.2 Measures", 2)
d.para(f"All constructs are measured with five-point agreement items (Appendix A). Table 1 shows the origin of each scale. The three pressure scales are adapted from the institutional-pressure measures of Teo et al. (2003), re-worded for budgeting and accrual reforms; substantive implementation, symbolic compliance and accountability are researcher-developed from the literature; maturity and usefulness combine adapted and new items. No scale is a published, validated instrument in this exact form, so we provide extensive validity evidence below. Content validity was assessed with the content validity ratio and index {ca('lawshe1975', 'polit2006')} ==[report the number of experts and the actual CVR/CVI values]==. Latent variables are never summed or averaged: every item is an indicator of its own latent variable only.", indent=True)
EN = {"COE": ("Coercive pressure", "Adapted"), "NOR": ("Normative pressure", "Adapted"), "MIM": ("Mimetic pressure", "Adapted"), "SUB": ("Substantive implementation", "Researcher-developed"), "SYM": ("Symbolic compliance", "Researcher-developed"),
      "DIG": ("Information-system maturity", "Adapted + new"), "USE": ("Usefulness of reported information", "Adapted + new"), "ACC": ("Financial accountability", "Researcher-developed")}
SRC = {"COE": "DiMaggio & Powell (1983); Teo et al. (2003)", "NOR": "DiMaggio & Powell (1983); Teo et al. (2003)", "MIM": "DiMaggio & Powell (1983); Oliver (1991); Teo et al. (2003)", "SUB": "Pollitt & Bouckaert (2017); Moynihan (2008); Christiaens et al. (2015)",
       "SYM": "Meyer & Rowan (1977); Bromley & Powell (2012); Modell (2009)", "DIG": "DeLone & McLean (2003); Dunleavy et al. (2006)", "USE": "Moynihan & Pandey (2010); Kroll (2015); DeLone & McLean (2003)", "ACC": "Bovens (2007); Bracci et al. (2015); Moore (1995)"}
d.table("Table 1. Constructs, number of items, origin of the scales and principal sources", ["Construct", "Items", "Origin", "Principal sources"], [(f"{EN[k][0]} ({k})", str(REL[k]["items"]), EN[k][1], SRC[k]) for k in EN], widths=[2500, 600, 1700, 4500], size=19)
d.heading("3.3 Estimation strategy", 2)
d.para(f"Models are estimated by maximum likelihood in semopy {ca('igolkina2020')}. Fit is judged jointly: CFI and TLI ≥ .95, RMSEA ≤ .06 and SRMR ≤ .08 {ca('hu1999')}; the χ² test is reported but, as is well known, over-rejects in samples of this size. Convergent validity uses standardized loadings, composite reliability (CR) and average variance extracted (AVE) {ca('fornell1981')}; discriminant validity uses the Fornell–Larcker criterion and the heterotrait–monotrait ratio {ca('henseler2015')}. Common-method bias is probed with Harman's single-factor test, a one-factor CFA and a common-latent-factor model {ca('podsakoff2003')}. Two latent interaction terms (maturity × substantive implementation; maturity × coercive pressure) are specified with matched-pair product indicators {ca('marsh2004')} and, as a robustness check, residual-centred indicators {ca('little2006')}. Indirect and conditional indirect effects are evaluated at −1, 0 and +1 SD of maturity with 2,000 nonparametric bootstrap draws and percentile intervals {ca('preacher2008')}; indices of moderated mediation follow {cn('hayes2015')}. Latent R² equals one minus the standardized residual variance, and the effect size of an interaction is f² from the change in R². Five competing structures are compared by chi-square difference tests and fit indices.")
