d.para(f"**Pressures and responses.** Coercive pressure raised both substantive implementation ({bz('SUB~COE')}; H1) and symbolic compliance ({bz('SYM~COE')}; H2). Normative pressure was the strongest predictor of substantive implementation ({bz('SUB~NOR')}; H3) and reduced symbolic compliance ({bz('SYM~NOR')}; H4). Mimetic pressure raised symbolic compliance ({bz('SYM~MIM')}; H5) and had no reliable effect on substantive implementation ({bz('SUB~MIM')}). The pattern is the signature of three different mechanisms: mandates produce activity of both kinds; professional norms push towards substance; templates push towards form.")
d.para(f"**Responses and outcomes.** Substantive implementation raised the usefulness of information ({bz('USE~SUB')}; H6) and accountability ({bz('ACC~SUB')}; H8), whereas symbolic compliance lowered usefulness ({bz('USE~SYM')}; H7) and accountability ({bz('ACC~SYM')}; H9). Usefulness was in turn positively related to accountability ({bz('ACC~USE')}; H10), which identifies it as a mechanism that carries part of the effect of both responses. Explained variance (latent R²) was {R['r2']['SUB']:.2f} for substantive implementation, {R['r2']['SYM']:.2f} for symbolic compliance, {R['r2']['USE']:.2f} for usefulness and {R['r2']['ACC']:.2f} for accountability, which in cross-sectional organisational research indicates substantial explanatory power.")
d.para(f"**Moderation.** Information-system maturity strengthened the effect of substantive implementation on usefulness ({bz('USE~INTS')}; H11) and weakened the effect of coercive pressure on symbolic compliance ({bz('SYM~INTC')}; H12). Effect sizes were small to moderate (f² = {R['f2_int']['USE']:.3f} for usefulness and {R['f2_int']['SYM']:.3f} for symbolic compliance), as is typical for interaction effects in non-experimental data. Figure 4 plots the conditional slopes: at low maturity the slope of usefulness on implementation is roughly {E['paths_unstd']['USE~SUB']['b'] + E['paths_unstd']['USE~INTS']['b']*R['sd_latent']['DIG']*-1:.2f}, at high maturity {E['paths_unstd']['USE~SUB']['b'] + E['paths_unstd']['USE~INTS']['b']*R['sd_latent']['DIG']:.2f}; the slope of symbolic compliance on coercion falls from {E['paths_unstd']['SYM~COE']['b'] - E['paths_unstd']['SYM~INTC']['b']*R['sd_latent']['DIG']:.2f} to {E['paths_unstd']['SYM~COE']['b'] + E['paths_unstd']['SYM~INTC']['b']*R['sd_latent']['DIG']:.2f}.")
d.figure(fig(4), "Figure 4. Conditional effects (simple slopes) of the two latent interactions at −1 SD, the mean and +1 SD of information-system maturity.", 15.2)
